---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FfmiCalculator from '../../../components/FfmiCalculator.astro';
import Faq from '../../../components/Faq.astro';

const schemas = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'FFMI Calculator',
    url: 'https://tdeecheck.com/calculators/ffmi-calculator/',
    applicationCategory: 'HealthApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Any',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Farah Akram', jobTitle: 'Dietitian' },
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is a good FFMI?',
    a: 'For men, a normalized FFMI of 18 to 20 is average for someone who does not train seriously, 20 to 22 reflects a regular lifter, and 22 to 24 is excellent and takes years of consistent training. Most recreational lifters should be pleased with anything above 21. Women typically score about 2 to 3 points lower at the same training level.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the natural limit for FFMI?',
    a: 'Research on drug tested and pre-steroid era athletes, starting with a well known 1995 study by Kouri and colleagues, found that a normalized FFMI around 25 is about the ceiling for natural men. A handful of genetic outliers reach 26 or slightly above. Sustained scores of 28 or more are considered very unlikely without performance enhancing drugs.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does normalized FFMI mean?',
    a: 'Raw FFMI penalizes tall people, because lean mass does not grow as fast as height squared. The normalized version adjusts your score to a standard height of 1.8 m (about 5 ft 11 in) using the correction FFMI + 6.1 x (1.8 - your height in m). If you are exactly 1.8 m tall, your raw and normalized scores are the same.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How accurate is this FFMI calculator?',
    a: 'The math is exact, but the result is only as good as your body fat input. A body fat estimate that is off by 3 points moves your FFMI by roughly a full point. DEXA scans and careful caliper measurements give the best inputs. Guessing your body fat from photos usually flatters you and inflates the score.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How can I increase my FFMI?',
    a: 'Only two levers exist: add lean mass or lose fat while keeping it. Progressive resistance training two to four times a week, protein around 1.6 to 2.2 g per kg of body weight, and either a small calorie surplus to build or a modest deficit to cut, chosen with the TDEE Calculator, are the whole recipe. Expect a few tenths of a point per year once you are past the beginner stage.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="FFMI Calculator - Fat Free Mass Index and Natural Limit"
  description="Free FFMI calculator. Work out your fat free mass index and normalized FFMI from weight, height, and body fat, and see where you sit on the natural scale."
  path="/calculators/ffmi-calculator/"
  schemas={schemas}
  crumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Calculators', path: '/calculators/' },
    { name: 'FFMI Calculator', path: '/calculators/ffmi-calculator/' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 pt-8">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink-900">FFMI Calculator</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
        BMI cannot tell muscle from fat. FFMI can. Enter your weight, height, and body fat percentage to get your Fat
        Free Mass Index, raw and height normalized, scored against the classic natural scale.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-8 max-w-4xl">
      <FfmiCalculator />
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">What FFMI measures, and why BMI cannot</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      FFMI, the Fat Free Mass Index, asks a smarter question than BMI. Instead of dividing your whole weight by your
      height squared, it first removes the fat. Your lean body mass, everything that is not fat, is divided by your
      height squared, which gives a score for how much muscle and other lean tissue you carry for your frame. A rugby
      player and an untrained office worker can share the same BMI of 28 while sitting at completely different FFMI
      scores, one muscular and one simply heavy. If you want that lean mass figure on its own first, our
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/calculators/lean-body-mass-calculator/">Lean Body Mass Calculator</a>
      works it out with three methods, and the <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/calculators/bmi-calculator/">BMI Calculator</a>
      shows the simpler screening number for comparison.
    </p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">How the calculation works</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Three steps. First, lean body mass: weight multiplied by (1 minus body fat percentage). An 80 kg man at 15
      percent body fat carries 68 kg of lean mass. Second, raw FFMI: lean mass divided by height in meters squared,
      so 68 divided by 1.8 squared (3.24) gives an FFMI of 21.0. Third, normalization: because taller people are
      slightly penalized by the squared height term, the score is adjusted to a standard height of 1.8 m by adding
      6.1 x (1.8 minus your height in meters). At exactly 1.8 m tall nothing changes, which is why the example above
      stays at 21.0, squarely in the above average band. The category shown with your result is based on this
      normalized score, the same convention used in the research the scale comes from.
    </p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Reading the FFMI scale honestly</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      The scale in your results runs from below average (under 18) through average, above average, and excellent,
      up to superior at 24 to 25.9. Beyond that the labels turn blunt on purpose. The 26 to 27.9 band is marked
      suspicious, and 28 plus is marked unlikely natural, because decades of data on tested athletes say natural
      muscle building essentially stops around 25 to 26. Treat those top bands as information, not accusation: a
      wrong body fat input, especially an underestimated one, can push an honest lifter a band too high. And
      remember the scale was built from male athletes. Women carry less lean mass at the same training age, and an
      advanced natural female lifter at 20 to 21 is roughly equivalent to a man at 23 to 24.
    </p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Using FFMI to set real goals</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      FFMI is most useful as a reality check on goal weights. If your target physique implies an FFMI of 27 and you
      train naturally, the plan needs adjusting, not more effort. Work backwards instead: pick a plausible FFMI for
      your training age, choose a body fat percentage you can actually hold, and the goal weight falls out of the
      math. Then feed that goal into the <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/">TDEE Calculator</a>
      for the calories to reach it, and use the
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/calculators/macro-calculator/">Macro Calculator</a> to set
      the protein that protects lean mass on the way. Gains of half a point a year are excellent progress for an
      experienced lifter, so judge your trend over seasons, not weeks.
    </p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Mistakes that distort your score</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      The most common error is a flattering body fat input. Smart scales can read several points low for muscular
      people and several points high for others, and photo comparison charts on the internet are notoriously
      optimistic. If your score lands in a band that surprises you, question the body fat number before the math.
      The second mistake is measuring in a strange state: dehydrated after a sauna or a hard cut, or loaded with
      water and glycogen after a refeed, your weight and apparent lean mass swing by a kilogram or more in a day.
      Measure in a normal, rested state. Third, remember that FFMI counts all lean tissue, including water and
      glycogen, so a beginner who gains fast in the first months of creatine and training has not broken the natural
      scale, they have filled their muscles with fluid. Judge your FFMI on consistent measurements taken months
      apart, not on your best morning ever.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6 rounded-xl bg-brand-50 border border-brand-100 px-5 py-4 text-sm text-ink-700">
      Reviewed by <strong>Farah Akram, Dietitian</strong>. Estimates only, not medical advice. See the
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/medical-disclaimer/">medical disclaimer</a>.
    </p>
    <Faq faqs={faqs} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
